      "problem": "$\\,$ $a$ is even or $b$ is odd.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$a$ is even or $b$ is odd\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a\\text{ is even or }b\\text{ is odd }) & ≡¬(a\\text{ is even }∨b\\text{ is odd }) \\\\  & ≡¬(a\\text{ is even })∧¬(b\\text{ is odd }) \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is odd }∧b\\text{ is even } \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is odd and }b\\text{ is even}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
